/*
  core/stressors/distraction_concurrent.spthy -- f_U for sigma2 ExternalDistraction, v2
  (CONCURRENCY-triggered), CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC.

  The v1 detector (external_distraction.spthy, kept for alex_blake_kdf) fires on ANY single
  pending step -- distraction as a free-floating environmental event. This v2 detector is closer
  to the construct it cites: Wickens' Multiple Resource Theory (2008) locates the degradation in
  COMPETING demands -- performance drops when a second task contends for the same resources. So
  the trigger is a DENSITY of two: the party has TWO distinct prompts presented (k=2, any action
  classes -- competition is cross-task by nature). It reads only the interface's Layer-1 prompts;
  no lexicon row (competition is action-independent).

  Counted at PRESENT time like sigma8/sigma9 (the prompts are on screen together). At onset it
  emits SetMask(P,'Careless') -- everything below SetMask is UNCHANGED. !StressEnable(P) gates per
  party; OneDistractPerParty bounds the onset; InequalityConcurrency is this file's private
  distinctness restriction (never co-include with a file defining the same name).
*/

rule Trigger_DistractionConcurrent [color=#eb5757]:
    [ !Step(P, p1, a1), !Step(P, p2, a2), !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ Distract(P), NeqC(p1, p2), SetMask(P,'Careless') ]->
    [ ]

restriction InequalityConcurrency:
  "All x #i. NeqC(x,x)@i ==> F"

restriction OneDistractPerParty:
  "All p #i #j. Distract(p)@i & Distract(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
